  {:id "K2" :repo "aiueos" :hyps ["bus3 answers ARP (E25's brief, done by the coordinator)" "why the LAN2 plane is silent in BOTH directions"]
   :state :landed-then-blocked :pr "https://github.com/kotoba-lang/aiueos/pull/316" :k16-tip "49d11f08c581"
   :measured "ARP: debug-init ends with a broadcast ARP REQUEST for 10.10.10.1 (a request addressed to the Mac is what makes a BSD stack record the sender; a gratuitous announcement only refreshes), returning 16+n if it did not go out; debug-poll answers requests for 10.10.10.2 before re-arming the RX descriptor and returns 16+n. store-tautology 234 forms / 0; wire-bytes 70 emissions / 42 entries / 0; KERNEL_OK fuel=1048576; QEMU MPRCD/33; image efc5d540 240640 B deployed 09:29."
   :root-cause "bus3 is cabled to en15's wire, not en8. Found by reading the PXE server's own log: DHCP_RX from 70:70:fc:0b:b6:31 (the board's SECOND NIC) arrives at a socket bound with IP_BOUND_IF to en15. Board->Mac frames carry en8's MAC (3c:18:a0:d5:82:a8) on a wire where en15 (80:69:1a:17:4f:15) is listening, so the NIC drops them -- debug-send's 0 was honest, the bytes did leave. Mac->board ARP for 10.10.10.2 goes out en8 by routing, where nothing answers. The Phase-1 `Z` on en8 was measured when the cable was elsewhere; the ADR carried a cabling fact as a property of the code for two days."
   :unblocked-partly "10:0x: the PXE server accepts a MAC LIST and was restarted with both K16 NICs (netlog moved to 7797 so it cannot fight the standalone receiver on 7777); it now offers to ...b6:31. The ADR's `root-only, never restart it` was WRONG -- measured: a free privileged UDP port binds as this user, and 67 returned EADDRINUSE not EACCES. No sudo was needed. Served image ce73e1b6 (ARP + broadcast destination + reply-to-sender). Rig check 9/9. The board still does not boot: it answers the offer with a plain DHCP DISCOVER (arch=65535 vendor='MSFT 5.0') in short bursts and is otherwise silent on both interfaces -- one power cycle is needed, and the loop that exists to make the button unnecessary is what put the button back."
   :answered-2026-09-08 "Six measurements ended the guessing. MAC loopback (TxConfig 17|18) returns the frame -- descriptors, DMA addresses and both engines are correct. PHYstatus is 0x0093 = link up, 1000 Mbps, full duplex, so the port has a live gigabit partner. 199 broadcasts from en8 and 199 from en15, across five boots each, produced no receive event; the greeting sent to 10.10.10.1 AND to 255.255.255.255 both reported sent and neither arrived. Everything this kernel owns is proven correct, and the wire carries nothing either way: the K16's second port is plugged into something that is not this Mac. The DHCP requests bearing bus3's MAC are frames the FIRMWARE composed with that address; they say nothing about which port they left by."
   :superseded-measurement "which interface the second NIC is on: en8 vs en15 Ipkts across one boot. If the offers to ...b6:31 leave on en15 while that NIC listens on en8, the server must reply on the interface the request arrived on."
   :blocked "the loop STOPPED at ~09:33: with bus3's link up the firmware netboots from it, the server accepts only bus2's MAC (PXE_EXPECTED_MAC), and the board no longer reaches bus2 -- 0 boots / 0 TFTP in 150 s after ~1.2 boots/min. efc5d540 has never been served. Owner action needed (ports 67/69 cannot be rebound from here): move the second cable to en8, or restart the PXE server accepting both MACs, or disable network boot on NIC2 in firmware."}
